          "session_id": "full-g-md-019",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "If your breathing gets worse, or you have trouble breathing, chest pain, or coughing up blood, seek urgent care now. A routine urgency check does not mean you are cleared. Also see a clinician about a cough that keeps going or keeps waking you, especially if your asthma or reflux treatment is no longer controlling it.\n\nThe guidance I found cannot tell us which condition is causing your night cough. Night-time worsening fits the asthma description well. The Asthma, condition summary says cough, wheeze, breathlessness and chest tightness often vary in intensity, are often worse at night or early in the morning, and can be set off by allergens, cold air, exercise or viral infection. It also says cough alone, especially at night, is a recognised presentation. The Asthma diagnosis and stepwise therapy guideline agrees. Asthma is confirmed by a symptom pattern plus evidence that airflow varies, usually measured with spirometry before and after a bronchodilator. A normal test when you feel well does not rule it out, and repeat testing while symptomatic is suggested.\n\nThe Gastroesophageal reflux disease, condition summary describes reflux as typically causing heartburn and acid regurgitation, often after meals, when bending forward, and when lying down. It lists chronic cough as a less typical presentation that is less specific, and it says cough can respond less predictably to acid suppression. The GERD initial management and alarm features guideline suggests lifestyle measures for night-time symptoms, such as raising the head of the bed, avoiding meals within two to three hours of lying down, stopping smoking and avoiding trigger foods.\n\nThe sources I checked did not say how asthma and reflux are linked, and they did not describe where experts disagree on the evidence. My search for that failed, so I cannot report it.\n\nThe GERD guideline also lists warning signs that need prompt investigation rather than a trial of treatment: difficulty or pain swallowing, unintended weight loss, vomiting blood or black stools, anaemia, persistent vomiting, a lump, and new symptoms at an older age. My urgency check did not retrieve a full red-flag list for your pattern, so this is the list I can offer.\n\nA clinician can help work out which condition is contributing. Bring a note of when the cough happens, what you were doing beforehand, whether it follows meals or lying down, and whether you have heartburn or regurgitation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
